y": 0.11}, "rating_margin": 14.25, "sigma_race": 5.2, "sigma_total": 5.64, "baseline_margin": 13.51, "analyst_adjustment": -1.01, "key_factors": ["Independent polls consistently show Hochul ahead by 9-19 points, with the latest Quinnipiac at 55-41", "All eight expert ratings place the race as Safe, Solid, or Likely Democratic", "Hochul's incumbency and New York's Democratic lean reinforce the polling edge", "Republican-sponsored polls showing 4-7 point margins and Blakeman's $13M fundraising are the main evidence against a blowout"], "rationale": "Every independent poll this fall shows Hochul leading by at least nine points, outside polling averages cluster near 12-13 points, and betting markets price her win odds around 95 percent. Only partisan-sponsored surveys show a single-digit race, which argues for a solid but not overwhelming Democratic edge.", "watch": "If nonpartisan polls begin converging toward the 4-7 point margins in Republican-sponsored surveys amid the affordability and energy-cost debate, the race would move toward Lean D.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kathy Hochul (D) is running."]}, "overview": "New York's governor's race favors incumbent Democrat Kathy Hochul over Republican Bruce Blakeman. Polling through the fall has shown Hochul ahead by double digits in most surveys, including a mid-October Quinnipiac poll putting the race at 55 to 41. Averages of public polls place her lead near 12 to 13 points, and betting markets give her roughly a 95 percent chance of winning. Every major race rating outfit ranks New York as Solid, Safe, or Likely Democratic.\n\nBlakeman has kept the race funded, raising more than $13 million, and Republican-sponsored polls show a narrower contest in the mid-to-high single digits. Affordability and energy costs have been the campaign's central clashes. Still, the state's Democratic lean, Hochul's incumbency, and consistent independent polling make an upset a low-probability outcome heading into the final s